A CSS rule answers two questions: **what should be targeted?** and **what should change?**

```css
p {
  color: #334155;
  line-height: 1.6;
}
```

`p` is the **selector**. Inside the braces are **declarations**. Each declaration contains a **property** such as `color`, a colon, a **value** such as `#334155`, and usually a semicolon.

One rule can contain several declarations. Keep each declaration on its own line while learning; the structure becomes easier to scan and debug.

**Line by line:** `p` is the selector, so the rule targets paragraph elements. The opening `{` begins the declaration block. `color: #334155;` sets the paragraph text color. `line-height: 1.6;` increases the vertical space between lines for readability. The closing `}` ends the rule. Reading the rule in that order makes it clear what is targeted and what each declaration changes.

A **descendant selector** uses a space to target an element only when it appears inside another matching element:

```css
.card p {
  color: #475569;
}
```

This targets paragraphs inside `.card`, not every paragraph on the page.

A **selector list** uses commas when multiple independent selectors should share the same declarations:

```css
h1,
h2 {
  font-family: Georgia, serif;
}
```

Do not confuse the two. A space means an ancestor–descendant relationship. A comma means separate selectors that share one rule.

